<<~ ahu #meme-header>>

! The Kumulipo — Liliʻuokalani Translation (1897)

Queen Liliʻuokalani's English translation of the Hawaiian creation chant, rendered while she was held under house arrest at ʻIolani Palace (1895) and completed at Washington, D.C., May 20, 1897. The koʻihonua (genealogical cosmogony) is attributed to the eighteenth-century chanter Keaulumoku and moves from the slime that established the earth, through sea and land life, to the genealogy of the ruling line.

Combined here from the per-era pages of the sacred-texts.com edition into one witness text, in reading order. Era headers and page markers `{p. N}` preserved.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #provenance>>

!! Provenance

Source: //An Account of the Creation of the World According to Hawaiian Tradition//, translated by Queen Liliʻuokalani of Hawaii. Lee and Shepard, Boston [1897]. Composition attributed to Keaulumoku (~1700).

Digital edition scanned at sacred-texts.com, April 2005, John Bruno Hare, redactor; public domain in the United States (published before 1923).

Retrieved 2026-06-21 via the Internet Archive Wayback Machine (snapshot `web.archive.org/web/20050413`) because sacred-texts.com now sits behind a Cloudflare interstitial. Source index: `https://sacred-texts.com/pac/lku/index.htm` (pages `lku00`–`lku18`).

The attribution notice from the original scan is retained inline in the Title Page section, per its terms.

<<~/ahu>>

